[{:db/id 17592186339078 :question/problem "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}-2(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}).\n$$\n\nIf ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ then general solution can be expressed in parametric form $(x(t),y(t)),$ where\n\n$$\ny(t)=(\\frac{2t-2\\ln|t|+C}{(t-1)^2})f(t)+g(t)\n$$\n\nand $C$ is an arbitrary constant. Find $f(t)+g(t).$\n\n*Hint: Under the given transformation, we have*\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{2x}{t-1}=\\frac{2}{t(t-1)}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Put $t=y'(x)$. The original equation becomes $y=xt^2-2t$, so $f(t)=t^2$ and $g(t)=-2t$.\n\nOn an interval where $t$ varies, differentiate with respect to $t$ and use $dy/dt=t\\,dx/dt$:\n\n$$t x'=t^2x'+2tx-2.$$\n\nThus $t(1-t)x'=2tx-2$, and for $t\\ne0,1$,\n\n$$x'+\\frac{2}{t-1}x=\\frac{2}{t(t-1)}.$$\n\nThe integrating factor is $(t-1)^2$, giving\n\n$$\\frac{d}{dt}\\big((t-1)^2x\\big)=2-\\frac{2}{t}.$$\n\nIntegration gives\n\n$$x(t)=\\frac{2t-2\\ln|t|+C}{(t-1)^2},\\qquad y(t)=t^2x(t)-2t.$$\n\nThese formulas satisfy both the original equation and $dy/dt=t\\,dx/dt$. Consequently,\n\n$$f(t)+g(t)=t^2-2t.$$\n\nThe parametric branch is interpreted locally where $dx/dt\\ne0$. Constant-slope solutions lost when dividing are $y=0$ ($t=0$) and $y=x-2$ ($t=1$). The original supplied hint and parametric coefficient are erroneous; neither was used in this derivation." :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
